    "text": "<p>A potentiometer wire&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>PQ</mi></math> of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> length is connected to a standard cell&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>. Another cell&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> of emf&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>02</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>&nbsp;is connected with a resistance &#39;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></math>&#39; and switch <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi></math> (as shown in figure). With switch&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi></math> open, the null position is obtained at a distance of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>49</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>&nbsp;from<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Q</mi></math>. The potential gradient in the potentiometer wire is :</p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/bbc8fc12-8205-4aaf-bd8f-d0ebe62ce442-image.png\" style=\"width: 260px; height: 179px;\" /></p>",
